= The January Uprising

On 4 January the government dismissed Emil Eichhorn, the Independent who had run the Berlin police since November. The next day hundreds of thousands of workers marched in protest. Armed groups occupied the offices of *Vorwärts* and the other papers of the newspaper district, and a Revolutionary Committee of the shop stewards, the Independents and our party declared the Ebert government deposed. Karl Liebknecht signed for us.

The Zentrale was not consulted. Rosa Luxemburg thinks the rising has no chance: the provinces are quiet and the garrison will not march. Leo Jogiches wants Liebknecht to leave the Committee at once. Karl Radek, who came from Moscow for our founding congress, urges a retreat. But the Berlin district, which follows the Left Radicals, is already in the buildings, and the crowds in the Siegesallee are waiting for orders.

Gustav Noske has been made commander-in-chief. He is gathering the Freikorps at Dahlem.

- @overthrow: Back the Revolutionary Committee and overthrow Ebert.
- @occupations: Defend the occupations, but no insurrection.
- @negotiate: Call for talks and a withdrawal from the buildings.

@overthrow
